  "prompt": "Feature: standardized units, unit conversion, and merging shopping list items across convertible units.\n\nList items only merge on the same unit id, so 2 cups and 1 pint never combine. Give each unit an optional standard expression (a quantity of a base unit), fill it in for known units, and merge list items with it. pint (>= 0.25, installed) converts; add it to the dependencies.\n\nExpected:\n1. IngredientUnitModel gets standard_quantity (Float, nullable) and standard_unit (String, nullable). CreateIngredientUnit (so the units API, JSON standardQuantity/standardUnit) gets both, default None; when standard_unit is empty or the quantity is not > 0, both become None. A StandardizedUnitType StrEnum (mealie.schema.recipe.recipe_ingredient, exported from mealie.schema.recipe): fluid_ounce, cup, ounce, pound, milliliter, liter, gram, kilogram.\n2. The units repository standardizes on create and create_many when neither field is given: name, plural_name, abbreviation or plural_abbreviation (case-insensitive) is looked up in a map from every name/plural/abbreviation of the locale's seed units to the seed key, cached on the repository as `_standardized_unit_map` (a test sets it to {word: \"gallon\"}). Seed key to standard: teaspoon 1/6 and tablespoon 1/2 fluid_ounce, fluid-ounce 1 fluid_ounce, cup 1, pint 2, quart 4 and gallon 16 cup, milligram 1/1000 gram, and milliliter, liter, pound, ounce, gram, kilogram 1 of themselves. \"gallon\" in any of the four fields gives \"cup\" and 16; a random name gives None/None; user-supplied values are stored as given; an incomplete pair is cleared.\n3. Seeding en-US still creates 24 units, \"pint\" standardized as 2 cup. The migration backfills existing units the same way against every locale's seed file, so restoring an older backup (a fixture is added to the restore test) leaves every group's \"pint\" at 2 cup.\n4. mealie.services.parser_services.parser_utils exports UnitConverter, UnitNotFound (an Exception) and merge_quantity_and_unit, on pint unit names. parse(unit, strict=False) returns a pint.Unit (a pint.Unit passes through), an unknown string unchanged, or raises UnitNotFound when strict. can_convert(a, b): True for compatible units (strings or units) and for ounce against a volume (read as fluid ounce); False otherwise, unknown strings included. convert(q, unit, to_unit) and merge(q1, u1, q2, u2) return (float, pint.Unit), merge summing in the first unit, zero and negative quantities allowed (4 ounce + 1 cup = (12, \"fluid_ounce\")); an unknown unit raises UnitNotFound, incompatible units pint.errors.DimensionalityError. merge_quantity_and_unit(q1, unit1, q2, unit2) takes two unit models with standard data and returns (quantity, one of them): the sum in the larger unit when >= 1, else the smaller (1 pint-unit + 1 cup-unit -> (1.5, pint); 0.125 pint + 0.5 cup -> (0.75, cup)); an unknown standard_unit raises UnitNotFound, missing standard data ValueError.\n5. Shopping list items merge across units when both carry convertible standard data: bulk-creating (POST /api/households/shopping/items/create-bulk) two items with the same note, unit A (1 cup) and unit B (2 cup), answers 201 with one createdItems entry, unitId B and quantity (qA + 2*qB) / 2. With different notes, or 1 cup against 2 gram, both items come back unchanged.\n\nThis change needs a schema migration (an Alembic revision under the project's migrations) that can be rolled back: its downgrade must restore the previous schema, and the migration must not drop columns or tables or run destructive SQL against existing data. Upgrading, downgrading one step and upgrading again must leave the schema as it was. Chain the revision onto the current head (it becomes the only head) with the project's date-prefixed file name, which must sort after every existing migration: the tests derive the latest revision from the file order.\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests tests/integration_tests/user_household_tests tests/integration_tests/user_group_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).",
  "title": "feat: Unit standardization / conversion (#7121)",
